  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 5.06 — Characteristic Classes\n\n5.05 left curvature as a gauge-dependent object: $F \\mapsto gFg^{-1}$ under a change of trivialisation. This notebook asks what survives that, and finds that the answer is **topology**.\n\n**Invariant polynomials.** A polynomial $P$ on $\\mathfrak g$ is invariant if $P(gXg^{-1}) = P(X)$ for all $g \\in G$. The coefficients of the characteristic polynomial are the obvious examples:\n\n$$\\det(\\lambda I + X) = \\sum_k \\lambda^{n-k}\\,\\sigma_k(X),$$\n\nwith $\\sigma_1 = \\operatorname{tr}X$, $\\sigma_n = \\det X$, and $\\operatorname{tr}(X^k)$ generating the same ring by Newton's identities.\n\n**Chern–Weil.** Substitute the curvature 2-form:\n\n$$P(F) \\quad\\text{is a } 2k\\text{-form, and}\\quad dP(F) = 0, \\quad [P(F)] \\text{ independent of } A.$$\n\nClosedness comes from the Bianchi identity of 5.05. Independence of $A$ comes from interpolating $A_t = A_0 + t(A_1 - A_0)$ and showing the difference is exact. So each invariant polynomial gives a **de Rham cohomology class of the base determined by the bundle alone** — a genuine topological invariant, extracted from a differential-geometric object that had no right to produce one.\n\n**The classes.**\n\n| class | defined by | detects |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chern $c_k$ | $\\det\\!\\big(I + \\tfrac{i}{2\\pi}F\\big)$, $G = U(n)$ | complex vector bundles |\n| Pontryagin $p_k$ | $\\det\\!\\big(I - \\tfrac{1}{2\\pi}F\\big)$, $G = O(n)$ | real bundles |\n| Euler $e$ | $\\mathrm{Pf}\\big(\\tfrac{1}{2\\pi}F\\big)$, $G = SO(2n)$ | orientation, and $\\chi$ |\n\nThe Euler class is the odd one out: it is a **Pfaffian**, not a polynomial in the characteristic-polynomial ring, defined only for even-rank oriented bundles, and squaring to the top Pontryagin class.\n\n**The payoff, in two dimensions.** For an oriented surface with the Levi-Civita connection, $F = -K\\,\\theta^1\\wedge\\theta^2$ (5.05), and the Euler class integrates to\n\n$$\\int_M e = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_M K\\,dA = \\chi(M).$$\n\nThat is **Gauss–Bonnet** (3.08) — now visible as the two-dimensional instance of a general theorem, and the reason a purely local quantity was constrained by a global one all along. In dimension $2n$ the same statement is the **Gauss–Bonnet–Chern theorem**.\n\n**Quantisation.** Because these classes come from integral cohomology, their integrals are *integers*. A curvature integral that could a priori be any real number is forced onto $\\mathbb{Z}$ by topology. Physically: magnetic charge is quantised (5.05's $U(1)$ bundle over $S^2$), and the instanton number of a Yang–Mills field is an integer.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* --- invariant polynomials: conjugation cannot be seen --- */\nXg : matrix([x11,x12],[x21,x22])$\nGg : matrix([g11,g12],[g21,g22])$\nCg : ratsimp( Gg . Xg . invert(Gg) )$\n\nprint(\"conjugation invariance, on a generic 2×2 X and generic invertible g:\")$\nprint(\"   tr(gXg⁻¹) − tr(X)   = \", ratsimp( mat_trace(Cg) - mat_trace(Xg) ))$\nprint(\"   tr((gXg⁻¹)²) − tr(X²) = \",\n      ratsimp( mat_trace(Cg.Cg) - mat_trace(Xg.Xg) ))$\nprint(\"   det(gXg⁻¹) − det(X) = \",\n      ratsimp( determinant(Cg) - determinant(Xg) ))$\nprint(\"   char poly difference = \",\n      ratsimp( determinant(lam*ident(2) + Cg)\n               - determinant(lam*ident(2) + Xg) ))$\nprint(\"   So every coefficient of det(λI + X) is an invariant polynomial.\")$\nprint(\"   These are the functions of curvature that survive a change of\")$\nprint(\"   gauge, because 5.05 showed F ↦ gFg⁻¹.\")$\n\n/* --- Newton's identities: power sums ↔ elementary symmetric --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Newton's identities, checked on a generic 3×3 matrix:\")$\nX3 : genmatrix( lambda([i,j], concat(m,i,j)), 3, 3)$\ncp : expand( determinant(lam*ident(3) + X3) )$\ns1 : ratsimp( coeff(cp, lam, 2) )$\ns2 : ratsimp( coeff(cp, lam, 1) )$\ns3 : ratsimp( coeff(cp, lam, 0) )$\np1 : ratsimp( mat_trace(X3) )$\np2 : ratsimp( mat_trace(X3 . X3) )$\np3 : ratsimp( mat_trace(X3 . X3 . X3) )$\nprint(\"   σ₁ − p₁                       = \", ratsimp(s1 - p1))$\nprint(\"   σ₂ − ½(p₁² − p₂)              = \", ratsimp(s2 - (p1^2 - p2)/2))$\nprint(\"   σ₃ − ⅙(p₁³ − 3p₁p₂ + 2p₃)     = \",\n      ratsimp(s3 - (p1^3 - 3*p1*p2 + 2*p3)/6))$\nprint(\"   det X − σ₃                    = \", ratsimp(determinant(X3) - s3))$\nprint(\"   So tr(F), tr(F²), tr(F³), … generate the same ring as the\")$\nprint(\"   characteristic-polynomial coefficients.  Chern–Weil can be stated\")$\nprint(\"   with either set.\")$\n\n/* --- the Pfaffian: the Euler class is NOT in that ring --- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"THE EULER CLASS IS DIFFERENT.  For a skew 2n×2n matrix, det is a\")$\nprint(\"perfect square and the Pfaffian is its square root:\")$\nSk2 : matrix([0, a],[-a, 0])$\nSk4 : matrix([0,a12,a13,a14],[-a12,0,a23,a24],\n             [-a13,-a23,0,a34],[-a14,-a24,-a34,0])$\npf2 : a$\npf4 : a12*a34 - a13*a24 + a14*a23$\nprint(\"   2×2:  det = \", determinant(Sk2), \"   Pf = \", pf2,\n      \"   det − Pf² = \", ratsimp(determinant(Sk2) - pf2^2))$\nprint(\"   4×4:  Pf = \", pf4)$\nprint(\"         det − Pf² = \", ratsimp( determinant(Sk4) - pf4^2 ))$\nprint(\"   Pf is a polynomial of degree n where det has degree 2n, so it is\")$\nprint(\"   genuinely a square root and NOT a polynomial in the traces.\")$\nprint(\"   That is why the Euler class exists only for oriented even-rank\")$\nprint(\"   bundles, and why e² = p_n rather than e being one of the p's.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "conjugation invariance, on a generic 2×2 X and generic invertible g:\n   tr(gXg⁻¹) − tr(X)   =  0\n   tr((gXg⁻¹)²) − tr(X²) =  0\n   det(gXg⁻¹) − det(X) =  0\n   char poly difference =  0\n   So every coefficient of det(λI + X) is an invariant polynomial.\n   These are the functions of curvature that survive a change of\n   gauge, because 5.05 showed F ↦ gFg⁻¹.\nNewton's identities, checked on a generic 3×3 matrix:\n   σ₁ − p₁                       =  0\n   σ₂ − ½(p₁² − p₂)              =  0\n   σ₃ − ⅙(p₁³ − 3p₁p₂ + 2p₃)     =  0\n   det X − σ₃                    =  0\n   So tr(F), tr(F²), tr(F³), … generate the same ring as the\n   characteristic-polynomial coefficients.  Chern–Weil can be stated\n   with either set.\nTHE EULER CLASS IS DIFFERENT.  For a skew 2n×2n matrix, det is a\nperfect square and the Pfaffian is its square root:\n   2×2:  det =  a^2    Pf =  a    det − Pf² =  0\n   4×4:  Pf =  a12*a34-a13*a24+a14*a23\n         det − Pf² =  0\n   Pf is a polynomial of degree n where det has degree 2n, so it is\n   genuinely a square root and NOT a polynomial in the traces.\n   That is why the Euler class exists only for oriented even-rank\n   bundles, and why e² = p_n rather than e being one of the p's."
     ]
    }
   ]
