  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- The total Chern class as an expansion --- */\nprint(\"total Chern class:  det(I + tX) = Σ tᵏ cₖ(X)\")$\nX2 : matrix([x11,x12],[x21,x22])$\nex : expand( determinant( ident(2) + tt*X2 ) )$\nprint(\"   2×2 : \", ex)$\nprint(\"      coefficient of t⁰ = \", coeff(ex,tt,0))$\nprint(\"      coefficient of t¹ = \", ratsimp(coeff(ex,tt,1)),\n      \"   = tr X ? \", is( ratsimp(coeff(ex,tt,1) - mat_trace(X2)) = 0 ))$\nprint(\"      coefficient of t² = \", ratsimp(coeff(ex,tt,2)),\n      \"   = det X ? \", is( ratsimp(coeff(ex,tt,2) - determinant(X2)) = 0 ))$\nprint(\"   With t = i/2π and X = F this reads c = 1 + c₁ + c₂, where c₁ is a\")$\nprint(\"   2-form and c₂ a 4-form.  On a SURFACE only c₁ can be non-zero,\")$\nprint(\"   for lack of room: c₂ would need four dimensions.\")$\n\n/* --- The Dirac monopole: a U(1) bundle over S² --- */\n/* Two patches.  In coordinates (θ, φ) a 1-form is [a_θ, a_φ].\n   A is written here as a REAL 1-form (the physicists' convention); the\n   mathematicians' u(1)-valued connection is i times it.  The factor of i\n   moves between the two lines below and fixes the SIGN of c₁, but not its\n   integrality, which is the point. */\nd1(al, xs) := ratsimp( diff(al[2], xs[1]) - diff(al[1], xs[2]) )$\nAN : [ 0,  nn/2 * (1 - cos(th)) ]$      /* good away from the south pole */\nAS : [ 0, -nn/2 * (1 + cos(th)) ]$      /* good away from the north pole */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"THE DIRAC MONOPOLE.  A U(1) connection on S², in two patches:\")$\nprint(\"   A^N = \", AN[2], \" dφ      (singular only at θ = π)\")$\nprint(\"   A^S = \", AS[2], \" dφ      (singular only at θ = 0)\")$\nprint(\"   F from the north patch = \", d1(AN, [th, ph]), \" dθ∧dφ\")$\nprint(\"   F from the south patch = \", d1(AS, [th, ph]), \" dθ∧dφ\")$\nprint(\"   difference = \", ratsimp( d1(AN,[th,ph]) - d1(AS,[th,ph]) ),\n      \"   — the curvature is global even though A is not.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   on the overlap, A^N − A^S = \", ratsimp(AN[2] - AS[2]), \" dφ\")$\nprint(\"   As u(1)-valued forms that is i·n dφ, and it must equal (dg)g⁻¹\")$\nprint(\"   for a transition function g : S¹ → U(1).  With g = e^{inφ} :\")$\nprint(\"      (dg)g⁻¹ = \", ratsimp( diff(exp(%i*nn*ph), ph)\n                                   * exp(-%i*nn*ph) ), \" dφ   ✓\")$\nprint(\"   Single-valuedness of g around the equator forces n ∈ ℤ.\")$\nprint(\"   THAT is the quantisation, and it is topological, not dynamical.\")$\n\nFN : d1(AN, [th, ph])$\ntot : integrate( integrate(FN, ph, 0, 2*%pi), th, 0, %pi )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   ∫_{S²} F = \", ratsimp(tot), \"   ⟹  F/2π integrates to \",\n      ratsimp(tot/(2*%pi)))$\nprint(\"   c₁ is ±this, the sign depending on the i-convention above.  The\")$\nprint(\"   INTEGER does not depend on it.  A curvature integral that could\")$\nprint(\"   a priori be any real number is pinned to ℤ by the bundle.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   n = 0 : the trivial bundle S² × U(1)\")$\nprint(\"   n = 1 : the Hopf bundle S³ → S², the generator\")$\nprint(\"   n = 2 : the tangent bundle of S² — and indeed the previous cell\")$\nprint(\"           found ∫K dA/2π = 2 for every sphere and every ellipsoid.\")$\nprint(\"           The Euler class of TS² and c₁ of the n = 2 line bundle\")$\nprint(\"           are the same class, which is why the two computations\")$\nprint(\"           agree.\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Beyond dimension 2:  for an SU(2) bundle over a 4-manifold the\")$\nprint(\"instanton number is  (1/8π²)∫ tr(F∧F) ∈ ℤ, built from the SAME\")$\nprint(\"construction with the invariant polynomial tr(X²) in place of tr(X).\")$\nprint(\"6.02's Einstein equations and this are two applications of one idea:\")$\nprint(\"curvature, made gauge-invariant, becomes topology.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
