     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "total Chern class:  det(I + tX) = Σ tᵏ cₖ(X)\n   2×2 :  tt^2*x11*x22+tt*x22-tt^2*x12*x21+tt*x11+1\n      coefficient of t⁰ =  1\n      coefficient of t¹ =  x22+x11    = tr X ?  true\n      coefficient of t² =  x11*x22-x12*x21    = det X ?  true\n   With t = i/2π and X = F this reads c = 1 + c₁ + c₂, where c₁ is a\n   2-form and c₂ a 4-form.  On a SURFACE only c₁ can be non-zero,\n   for lack of room: c₂ would need four dimensions.\nTHE DIRAC MONOPOLE.  A U(1) connection on S², in two patches:\n   A^N =  (nn*(1-cos(th)))/2  dφ      (singular only at θ = π)\n   A^S =  -((nn*(cos(th)+1))/2)  dφ      (singular only at θ = 0)\n   F from the north patch =  (nn*sin(th))/2  dθ∧dφ\n   F from the south patch =  (nn*sin(th))/2  dθ∧dφ\n   difference =  0    — the curvature is global even though A is not.\n   on the overlap, A^N − A^S =  nn  dφ\n   As u(1)-valued forms that is i·n dφ, and it must equal (dg)g⁻¹\n   for a transition function g : S¹ → U(1).  With g = e^{inφ} :\n      (dg)g⁻¹ =  %i*nn  dφ   ✓\n   Single-valuedness of g around the equator forces n ∈ ℤ.\n   THAT is the quantisation, and it is topological, not dynamical.\n   ∫_{S²} F =  2*%pi*nn    ⟹  F/2π integrates to  nn\n   c₁ is ±this, the sign depending on the i-convention above.  The\n   INTEGER does not depend on it.  A curvature integral that could\n   a priori be any real number is pinned to ℤ by the bundle.\n   n = 0 : the trivial bundle S² × U(1)\n   n = 1 : the Hopf bundle S³ → S², the generator\n   n = 2 : the tangent bundle of S² — and indeed the previous cell\n           found ∫K dA/2π = 2 for every sphere and every ellipsoid.\n           The Euler class of TS² and c₁ of the n = 2 line bundle\n           are the same class, which is why the two computations\n           agree.\nBeyond dimension 2:  for an SU(2) bundle over a 4-manifold the\ninstanton number is  (1/8π²)∫ tr(F∧F) ∈ ℤ, built from the SAME\nconstruction with the invariant polynomial tr(X²) in place of tr(X).\n6.02's Einstein equations and this are two applications of one idea:\ncurvature, made gauge-invariant, becomes topology."
