  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$P(F) \\text{ closed}, \\quad [P(F)] \\text{ independent of } A \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_M K\\,dA = \\chi(M)$$\n\n**What survives a gauge transformation.** $\\operatorname{tr}X$, $\\operatorname{tr}(X^2)$, $\\det X$ and every coefficient of $\\det(\\lambda I + X)$ were checked conjugation-invariant on a generic $2\\times2$ matrix with a generic invertible $g$ — all differences identically zero. Since 5.05 showed $F \\mapsto gFg^{-1}$, these are exactly the functions of curvature that do not depend on the trivialisation.\n\nNewton's identities were verified on a generic $3\\times3$ matrix ($\\sigma_1 = p_1$, $\\sigma_2 = \\tfrac12(p_1^2-p_2)$, $\\sigma_3 = \\tfrac16(p_1^3-3p_1p_2+2p_3)$, all differences zero), so the traces and the characteristic-polynomial coefficients generate the same ring — Chern–Weil can be stated with either.\n\n**The Euler class is genuinely different.** For skew matrices $\\det = \\mathrm{Pf}^2$, verified in ranks 2 and 4 ($\\mathrm{Pf}_4 = a_{12}a_{34} - a_{13}a_{24} + a_{14}a_{23}$, difference zero). The Pfaffian has degree $n$ where $\\det$ has degree $2n$, so it is a genuine square root and *not* a polynomial in the traces. Hence the Euler class exists only for oriented even-rank bundles, and $e^2 = p_n$ rather than $e$ being one of the $p$'s.\n\n**Gauss–Bonnet, four ways.**\n\n| surface | $\\tfrac{1}{2\\pi}\\int K\\,dA$ | $\\chi$ |\n|---|---|---|\n| sphere, radius $R_0$ | $2$ — $R_0$ cancels in the integrand | $2$ |\n| torus of revolution | $0$ | $0$ |\n| ellipsoid $(1,1,2)$ | $2.0$ (numerical, error $\\sim 10^{-12}$) | $2$ |\n| closed hyperbolic, genus $g$ | $2-2g$ | $2-2g$ |\n\nThe ellipsoid is the one that makes the point. Its $K$ is not constant — larger at the poles, smaller at the equator — and the integral does not notice. 4.06 found the torus with $K$ positive outside and negative inside; this says the two cancel **exactly**, and no reshaping of the tube can disturb it.\n\n**A slip worth recording.** A first version of the integrand divided by $W = EG - F^2$ where it needed $W^{3/2}$ (each of $L$, $M$, $N$ carries a $1/\\sqrt W$ from the unit normal). The sphere then returned $\\pi R_0^2/2$. That is *obviously* wrong for a reason that has nothing to do with checking the algebra: $\\chi$ cannot depend on the radius. Dimensional reasoning caught it before the algebra did.\n\n**Quantisation, from two patches.** The Dirac monopole connection $A^N = \\tfrac n2(1-\\cos\\theta)d\\varphi$, $A^S = -\\tfrac n2(1+\\cos\\theta)d\\varphi$ gives the *same* $F = \\tfrac n2\\sin\\theta\\,d\\theta\\wedge d\\varphi$ on the overlap — difference zero. The curvature is global even though the potential is not. On the equator $A^N - A^S = n\\,d\\varphi$, which must be $(dg)g^{-1}$ for a transition function $g: S^1 \\to U(1)$; with $g = e^{in\\varphi}$ that is $in\\,d\\varphi$, and single-valuedness forces $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nThen $\\int_{S^2}F = 2\\pi n$ exactly, so $F/2\\pi$ integrates to an integer. **A curvature integral that could a priori be any real number is pinned to $\\mathbb{Z}$ by the bundle**, with no dynamics involved.\n\nAnd the two computations meet: $n = 2$ is the tangent bundle of $S^2$, whose Euler class integrates to $2$ — the same number the ellipsoid gave.\n\n## Stage 5 closes here\n\nIt began by linearising a group at the identity (5.01), rebuilt the group from that linearisation (5.02), put a metric on it so that curvature became a bracket (5.03), found that one sign in that bracket separates spherical from hyperbolic (5.04), moved the connection off the manifold and onto a bundle where general relativity and electromagnetism became one construction (5.05), and ended by extracting from curvature a set of integers that no deformation can change.\n\nThe thread from 1.01 holds: **the cross product of first-year vector algebra is $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, and $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$'s bracket is the curvature of the 3-sphere.**\n\n## Next\n\n**Stage 6 — Geometry in the Wild** is a menu of applications, largely independent of one another. 6.01 and 6.02 take the machinery to Lorentzian signature and general relativity, where 4.05's Schwarzschild calculation gets finished properly; 6.03 does symplectic geometry and mechanics; 6.06 rebuilds curvature on triangle meshes, where Gauss–Bonnet holds exactly rather than approximately.\n\n## References\n\n- Milnor, J.W. and Stasheff, J.D. (1974) *Characteristic Classes*. Annals of Mathematics Studies 76. Princeton: Princeton University Press. The standard account, and unusually readable; §14 for Chern classes, §9 for Stiefel–Whitney, Appendix C for the Chern–Weil construction used here.\n- Chern, S.-S. (1944) 'A simple intrinsic proof of the Gauss–Bonnet formula for closed Riemannian manifolds', *Annals of Mathematics*, 45(4), pp. 747–752. The paper that made Gauss–Bonnet an $n$-dimensional theorem, and the reason it is Gauss–Bonnet–**Chern**.\n- Nakahara, M. (2003) *Geometry, Topology and Physics*. 2nd edn. Bristol: Institute of Physics. Chapters 10–11. The monopole and instanton computations of the last cell in full.\n- Bott, R. and Tu, L.W. (1982) *Differential Forms in Algebraic Topology*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 82. New York: Springer. Chapters 3–4, for the de Rham side of why $[P(F)]$ is well defined.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9). Chapter 9 proves Gauss–Bonnet in the form 3.08 used, which is the $n = 2$ case of everything above.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The Chern–Weil theorem itself is not proved here — closedness and connection-independence are quoted, and what is computed is the resulting numbers, which is where the checks against 3.08 and 4.07 bite.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
}